# XCU at
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Every job is scheduled for December 2035, so none can run during a test,
# and every job a test submits is removed on every exit path with at -r.
# The tests run with TZ=UTC0 unless a zone is the point. A system on which the
# user is not allowed to use at (the XSI access files) makes the submission
# fail; the tests then report UNRESOLVED, since a refusal for that reason is
# permitted. The commands of a job are "true". Mail (-m) and the run of a job
# are not exercised.

assertion  XCU.at.001
anchor     utilities/at.html#STDERR
anchor     utilities/at.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/at.html#OPTIONS
class      required
says       at -t 203512091200 with commands on standard input submits a job and
           exits with status zero; standard error then holds a line of the form
           "job ID at DATE" with ID made only of letters, digits and periods and
           DATE the scheduled time in the form of date's POSIX-locale format
           (weekday, month, day, time, year: Sun Dec 9 12:00:00 2035); nothing
           is written to standard output.
test       t/001-submit-message.sh

assertion  XCU.at.002
anchor     utilities/at.html#STDOUT
anchor     utilities/at.html#OPTIONS
class      required
says       at -l writes a line for each of the invoking user's jobs holding the
           job ID, a tab and the scheduled time in the same form as the
           submission message; at -l with job IDs lists only those jobs.
test       t/002-list.sh

assertion  XCU.at.003
anchor     utilities/at.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/at.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       Two jobs submitted by one user get different IDs; at -r ID removes the
           job with exit status zero, after which at -l no longer lists it
           while the other job stays listed.
test       t/003-remove.sh

assertion  XCU.at.004
anchor     utilities/at.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/at.html#CONSEQUENCES OF ERRORS
class      required
says       at -f FILE takes the job's commands from FILE (exit status zero and
           a job is scheduled), and when FILE does not exist at exits with a
           status above zero and schedules no job.
test       t/004-file-option.sh

assertion  XCU.at.005
anchor     utilities/at.html#OPERANDS
class      required
says       The timespec operands noon, midnight, 24-hour times, 12-hour times
           with am or pm, a month name with day and year, and the increments
           "+ N weeks", "+ N days" and "next day" give the expected scheduled
           times, and a time with the zone name utc is shown in the zone of TZ.
test       t/005-timespec.sh

assertion  XCU.at.006
anchor     utilities/at.html#CONSEQUENCES OF ERRORS
anchor     utilities/at.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
says       An invalid time (an hour of 25, a word that is no time, or a -t
           argument that is not a date) gives a status above zero and no job
           is scheduled.
test       t/006-invalid-time.sh

assertion  XCU.at.007
anchor     utilities/at.html#OPTIONS
class      required
says       Jobs go to queue a unless -q says otherwise: a job submitted without
           -q is listed by at -l -q a and not by at -l -q b, and one submitted
           with -q a is listed by at -l -q a.
test       t/007-queues.sh

assertion  XCU.at.008
anchor     utilities/at.html#EXIT STATUS
anchor     utilities/at.html#OPTIONS
class      required
says       at -r of an ID that names no job exits with a status above zero.
test       t/008-remove-unknown.sh

assertion  XCU.at.009
anchor     utilities/at.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/at.html#OPTIONS
class      untestable
says       A job runs at its time in a separate shell with the environment
           variables, working directory and umask of the submission, mail is
           sent after the job with -m (or the job's output when not redirected),
           and the SHELL variable selects the interpreter.
reason     Running a job needs the scheduler daemon and a wait for the job's
           time, which cannot be bounded for a test; the jobs of these tests
           are placed years ahead on purpose.
